WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.375
SAURABH GUPTA:
Hello, and welcome

00:00:01.375 --> 00:00:03.640
to Understanding Audience
Experiences on Google Cloud

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:05.010
Platform.

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:06.540
My name is Saurabh
Gupta, and I'm

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.310
a customer engineer
at Google Cloud

00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:10.500
for Media and Entertainment.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:12.180
Media streaming has
a global audience

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:15.180
with wide variances in
device types, user locations,

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:17.970
connection bandwidth,
and user behaviors.

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:20.400
Today, I'll be talking
about large-scale media

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:23.760
analytics for digital streaming
and how Google Cloud's products

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:27.090
can make it easy to ingest,
analyze, and get insights

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:30.690
at scale from millions of
users watching your content.

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:32.610
This demo shows the
power of Google Cloud's

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:35.640
fully-managed Enterprise
Data Warehouse, BigQuery,

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:38.070
along with Google Cloud's
fully-managed Enterprise Data

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:41.640
Visualization tool called Looker
to explore the media audience

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:45.240
experience and answer the
question how many people are

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:47.490
finishing their videos.

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:49.410
Over the past few days,
members of my team

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:51.910
have been watching
various short movies.

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:53.910
Events are emitted from
the Shaka player running

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:57.090
in the browser being used for
these movie watching sessions.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:59.220
These events are
transmitted to an ingest end

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:02.910
point running on the Cloud
Run Serverless platform.

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:05.220
The events are
enqueued onto PubSub,

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:08.550
which is a fully-managed
global event streaming system.

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:11.490
Dataflow is a unified stream
and batch data processing

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:13.710
service based on Apache Beam.

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:16.530
Dataflow uses the events stream
from PubSub as a data source

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:19.350
and inserts the
data into BigQuery.

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:21.750
Looker is then used
to visualize the data

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:23.640
and get meaningful
insights from it.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:25.170
So over to the demo.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:33.170
Here, you can see a simulation
of a streaming services video

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:35.180
library, just as
what a streaming

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:38.750
customer might be expected to
see in their streaming service.

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:41.990
As a user, I can select
any movie to watch.

00:01:41.990 --> 00:01:46.700
Before we begin, let us activate
the browser's debug window,

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:48.380
so we can see the
events that are

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:50.460
being emitted for analytics.

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:54.800
Let me now select one of my
favorite movies, "Caminandes."

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:57.110
The video starts playing
in the video page

00:01:57.110 --> 00:02:01.680
and a sample Preroll ad
starts before the video.

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:04.610
You can see the different ad
quartile events being emitted.

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:09.530
Let me click on the ad, which
takes me to the ad's details

00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:11.480
page, where a
customer would likely

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:15.260
see the details of the ad
they've just clicked on.

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:18.200
Let's return to the video
and continue playing.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:21.690
As expected, other
events continue to fire.

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:25.540
Let me mute, and you can see
that event being emitted.

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:27.580
And let me pause the video.

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.690
And again, all these events
are streaming directly

00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:30.830
into BigQuery.

00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:33.890
So let's take a
look at BigQuery.

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:38.210
Here is where the events would
land in a specific data set.

00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:40.360
I can run a sample query
to see these events land

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:41.990
in BigQuery in near real time.

00:02:44.953 --> 00:02:46.370
This query shows
me all the events

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:48.320
that have accumulated
in the past 1 minute,

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:53.630
including the events
related to pausing the ad

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:58.820
and pausing the video as I
issued them on the player.

00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:01.910
All of these events can
be analyzed in Looker

00:03:01.910 --> 00:03:03.770
using visual dashboards.

00:03:03.770 --> 00:03:06.620
Let's take a look at that.

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:10.820
Here is the Media Event
Analytics dashboard

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:14.232
that we've created in Looker
based on the data in BigQuery.

00:03:14.232 --> 00:03:16.190
Up here, you can see the
total number of events

00:03:16.190 --> 00:03:18.200
that we have logged,
the number of sessions

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:20.090
we've encountered
this month, and, most

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:21.830
importantly, the
viewership funnel,

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:24.530
which shows us the
progression of viewership

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:26.940
from one quartile of
the video to the next.

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:30.470
So we can see the number
of starts, mid-points,

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:32.030
and completion of videos.

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:36.110
We can also see where
customers are watching

00:03:36.110 --> 00:03:39.740
the videos in a map view.

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:42.410
Additionally, we can also
see the device breakdown

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:45.110
and get session level details.

00:03:45.110 --> 00:03:47.720
Further down, we can also
break down the ad funnel

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:50.100
in terms of how many
ads were started,

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:53.180
how many were completed, and
how many were clicked through.

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:57.290
We can also get details
on a particular asset

00:03:57.290 --> 00:03:59.570
and drill into that.

00:03:59.570 --> 00:04:02.270
So here, let's select
"Sintel" and see

00:04:02.270 --> 00:04:06.980
the details of what this
specific asset is collecting

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:09.410
from an analytic standpoint.

00:04:09.410 --> 00:04:13.790
Again, the data that we got at
the coarse-grained gross level,

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:16.339
we can now drill down
at a finer level and get

00:04:16.339 --> 00:04:18.649
details about this
specific video

00:04:18.649 --> 00:04:21.480
in terms of how many events
were issued against it,

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:24.850
how many sessions,
the viewership funnel,

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:30.200
the completion rates, devices,
ads, sessions, and also

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:33.420
geographic distribution.

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:37.660
Let us now return
to the presentation.

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:39.400
As you just saw,
BigQuery and Looker

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:41.832
provide a unified view
of data across ads,

00:04:41.832 --> 00:04:44.290
players, and other sources of
data regarding your streaming

00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:45.700
platform.

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:48.130
BigQuery and Looker
offer self-service access

00:04:48.130 --> 00:04:50.530
to key insights for the
various stakeholders,

00:04:50.530 --> 00:04:54.040
thus allowing better and
more timely decision making.

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:56.020
And since these are
fully-managed serverless

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:58.660
components, you're freed
from the overhead of managing

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:02.080
infrastructure and can focus
on running your business.

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:05.020
BigQuery and Looker make
a powerful combination

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:08.260
for streaming media analytics,
allowing you to unify analytics

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:11.040
across ads, content,
viewers, and other sources,

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:13.690
derive meaningful
insights in minimal time

00:05:13.690 --> 00:05:16.240
leading to deep understanding
of the audience experience

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:18.100
for actionable outcomes.

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:20.110
Thanks for watching
Understanding the User

00:05:20.110 --> 00:05:22.520
Experience on Google Cloud demo.

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:24.535
Please visit
cloud.google.com/solutions/media

00:05:24.535 --> 00:05:30.940
entertainment for more details
and other solutions for media

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:31.990
on Google Cloud.

00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:35.340
[MUSIC PLAYING]

